<figure class="wp-block-image alignright is-resized is-style-rounded"><img decoding="async" width="600" height="600" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg" alt="Aysegul Sanford from front view." class="wp-image-84391" style="width:200px;height:200px" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg 600w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-400x400.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-150x150.jpg 150w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-500x500.jpg 500w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-300x300.jpg 300w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-96x96.jpg 96w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-378x378.jpg 378w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-276x276.jpg 276w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg"></figure>



<p>Growing up in T&uuml;rkiye, we did not celebrate Thanksgiving, so I learned how to cook a turkey as an adult after moving to the U.S. more than 20 years ago. Over the years, watching more experienced cooks and learning from a few dry turkeys myself, I discovered one very important truth:</p>



<p><strong>There is a tiny window between juicy perfection and dry disappointment.</strong></p>



<p>So I started focusing on the techniques that truly keep turkey moist. One option is using a <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/turkey-injection-recipe/">Turkey Injection</a> to add seasoning and moisture inside the meat, but I also wanted an option that was easier and required no special tools.</p>



<p>That is where this compound butter, which I specifically created for turkey, comes in.</p>



<p>In the simplest terms, it is softened butter mixed with herbs, garlic, and spices that slowly melts under the skin as the turkey roasts, keeping the meat juicy and helping the skin turn beautifully golden and crisp.</p>



<p>To me, this make-ahead friendly recipe is a simple shortcut with big rewards. And in this post, I will walk you through everything you need to know, from the herbs and spices to use to the easiest way to spread it under the skin so you can confidently cook a tender, flavorful, perfectly browned turkey that everyone will love.</p>

</div>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-ingredients-you-ll-need-with-substitution-ideas">Ingredients You&rsquo;ll Need With Substitution Ideas</h2>



<p>The key ingredients in this compound butter recipe have the perfect balance of fresh herbs, citrus, and garlic, resulting in a perfectly seasoned turkey. You can find the exact measurements and the full list in the recipe card below, but here are a few important notes from my testing:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound.jpg" alt="Ingredients for butter mixture for turkey." class="wp-image-78427" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound-350x525.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound-768x1152.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound-1024x1536.jpg 1024w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound-400x600.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound-150x225.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-butter-compound.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Unsalted butter:</strong> I like to use unsalted butter to control the amount of salt. With that said, you can use salted butter and reduce the salt to &frac34; teaspoon.</p>



<p><strong>Fresh herbs (or dried herbs):</strong> Fresh herbs will give you the best flavor. I like adding fresh rosemary, fresh thyme, and fresh sage, but you can also incorporate soft herbs like fresh parsley or chives.</p>


<div class="su-note" style="border-color:#d9d8d7;border-radius:1px;-moz-border-radius:1px;-webkit-border-radius:1px;"><div class="su-note-inner su-u-clearfix su-u-trim" style="background-color:#f3f2f1;border-color:#ffffff;color:#000;border-radius:1px;-moz-border-radius:1px;-webkit-border-radius:1px;">



<p>While I recommend using fresh herbs, dried ones can also be used as an alternative. Use the chart below when substituting:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><tbody><tr><td>For 1 Stick (4 oz) of Butter</td><td>Dried Herb/Seasoning Equivalent</td></tr><tr><td>1 tbsp fresh rosemary</td><td>1 tsp dried rosemary</td></tr><tr><td>1 tbsp fresh rhyme</td><td>1 tsp dried thyme</td></tr><tr><td>1 tsp fresh sage</td><td>1/3 tsp dried sage</td></tr><tr><td>2 cloves fresh garlic</td><td>1/2 tsp garlic powder</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>


</div></div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-make-compound-butter-for-turkey-step-by-step">How to Make Compound Butter for Turkey (Step-by-Step)</h2>



<p>Once your ingredients are ready, this recipe can be prepared in under 5 minutes. Here&rsquo;s how to make it step by step:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin.jpg" alt="A collage of images showing how to make butter seasoning for turkey." class="wp-image-78428" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/seasoning-under-turkey-skin.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Mix butter, herbs, and garlic:</strong> In a small bowl, combine softened butter, rosemary, thyme, sage, garlic, lemon zest, Kosher salt, and black pepper. Mash the herbs into the butter with the back of a fork or a rubber spatula until well combined, for about 1 minute. If you&rsquo;re using salted butter, just reduce the Kosher salt slightly.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1499" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey.jpg" alt="A collage of images showing how to roll compound butter for thanksgiving turkey." class="wp-image-78429" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey-350x437.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey-768x959.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey-400x500.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey-150x187.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/recipe-for-compound-butter-for-turkey.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Use immediately or shape into a log:</strong> At this point, your turkey compound butter recipe is ready to use. You can use it right away or roll it up for later use.&nbsp;</p>



<p>If you are planning to use it later, place the butter on a sheet of parchment paper or plastic wrap. Then shape it into a log, wrap it tightly, and refrigerate for at least 3 hours.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> I like to twist the ends of the parchment like a candy wrapper. It helps the butter keep its shape and makes it super easy to slice later.</p>

</div></div>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-make-ahead-storage-and-freezing-tips">Make-Ahead, Storage, and Freezing Tips</h2>



<p>One of the reasons I love using this compound butter for Thanksgiving turkey is that it can be made ahead of time, and it stores well. Here&rsquo;s how I do it:</p>



<p><strong>Make Ahead and Storage:</strong> Prepare the butter as directed, then shape it into a log and wrap it tightly in parchment or plastic wrap. I like to do a double wrap (plastic wrap followed by an airtight container) to keep it fresh and prevent it from picking up any fridge odors. It will keep well in the refrigerator for up to 5 days.</p>



<p><strong>Freezing:</strong> Follow the same wrapping steps and store the butter in a freezer-safe bag or container for up to 3 months.</p>



<p><strong>Thawing:</strong> When you&rsquo;re ready to use it, take it out of the freezer and let it sit at room temperature for about 1 hour, or until it softens enough to slice and spread.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1800" data-pin-description="The easiest garlic and herb compound butter to make for your Thanksgiving turkey." data-pin-title="Turkey Compound Butter" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe.jpg" alt="Sliced Thanksgiving turkey compound butter with a knife on the side." class="wp-image-78367" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe-350x525.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe-768x1152.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe-1024x1536.jpg 1024w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe-400x600.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe-150x225.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-compound-butter-recipe.jpg"></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-apply-compound-butter-under-turkey-skin-step-by-step">How to Apply Compound Butter Under Turkey Skin (Step-by-Step)</h2>



<p>Now that our compound butter is ready, it&rsquo;s time to put it to use. If it&rsquo;s your first time spreading compound butter under the turkey skin, don&rsquo;t worry, as it&rsquo;s easier than it looks.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1499" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter.jpg" alt="A person showing how to place butter and herbs under skin for turkey." class="wp-image-78430" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter-350x437.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter-768x959.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter-400x500.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter-150x187.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/turkey-seasoning-butter.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Dry the skin:</strong> Let your turkey rest at room temperature for about an hour before you start. This is critical because cold skin is rigid and prone to tearing, but a rested bird&rsquo;s skin will loosen up and be much more forgiving.</p>



<p>Set your turkey on a clean cutting board and pat the skin dry with paper towels on all sides. This will help the butter stick better and ensure that it crisps up beautifully in the oven.&nbsp;</p>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Loosen the skin: </strong>Locate the neck opening and gently slide your fingers between the skin and meat to loosen it.<strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>



<p>If you feel resistance, stop immediately. Instead of forcing separation, gently wiggle your fingers so that it is loose enough to push the butter under the skin.</p>



<p><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Apply the butter: </strong>Take about two tablespoons of your turkey butter herb rub and spread it under the skin, focusing on the breast and thighs. Spread it as evenly as possible by massaging the entire bird, including both the under and outer skin.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Don&rsquo;t worry about it being perfect, as the butter will melt while the turkey roasts.</p>



<p><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Roast the turkey: </strong>Place the prepared turkey in a roasting pan with aromatics such as onions, carrots, and celery.</p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-compound-butter-vs-dry-rub-which-is-best-for-your-turkey">Compound Butter vs. Dry Rub: Which is Best for Your Turkey?</h2>



<p>I&rsquo;ll be honest, when it comes to seasoning the whole bird, this turkey compound butter recipe is my go-to. However, it is not the only way. If you are wondering whether to use this herbed butter or opt for my <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/turkey-rub-recipe/">Homemade Turkey Rub</a>, the choice comes down to your goal:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Choose Compound Butter for:</strong>
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Maximum Moisture and Juiciness:</strong> The fat melts <em>beneath</em> the skin, self-basting the white meat throughout the long cooking time, which is key to a tender breast.</li>



<li><strong>Golden-Brown Skin:</strong> The butter is brushed over the skin, which helps brown the turkey beautifully and develop a rich, buttery flavor.</li>



<li><strong>Make-Ahead Simplicity:</strong> You can prepare this compound butter days in advance, saving time on Thanksgiving day.</li>
</ul>
</li>



<li><strong>Choose a Turkey Dry Rub for:</strong>
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Ultra-Crispy Skin:</strong> A dry rub acts as a dry brine, helping to draw moisture from the skin and give you an extra-crispy exterior.</li>



<li><strong>Convenience &amp; Speed:</strong> If you are short on time and need a quick way to add seasoning, the dry rub is much faster to apply than the compound butter.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-faqs">FAQs</h2>



<div class="schema-faq wp-block-yoast-faq-block"><div class="schema-faq-section" id="faq-question-1763007916734"><strong class="schema-faq-question">How much compound butter do I need for turkey?</strong> <p class="schema-faq-answer">Over the years of using this compound butter when making our Thanksgiving turkey, I learned that 8 tablespoons (1 stick or 4 ounces) is enough for a bird that is up to 12 pounds, which is about &frac34; tablespoon per pound. The important thing here is even distribution under and over the skin without overdoing it.<br>For a larger bird, you can easily multiply the recipe. If your turkey is between 12 and 18 pounds, multiply the ingredients by 1.5 times.</p> </div> <div class="schema-faq-section" id="faq-question-1763007933008"><strong class="schema-faq-question">When should I butter my turkey?</strong> <p class="schema-faq-answer">It&rsquo;s best to butter the turkey right before roasting. Applying the butter just before it goes in the oven helps the skin get crispy while the meat stays juicy and tender throughout cooking.</p> </div> <div class="schema-faq-section" id="faq-question-1763007959307"><strong class="schema-faq-question">How to make butter stick to turkey?</strong> <p class="schema-faq-answer">If the butter isn&rsquo;t spreading easily, it&rsquo;s usually because the turkey skin is damp or the butter is too cold. I like to take the turkey out of the fridge about 1 hour before roasting and pat the skin completely dry with paper towels before adding the butter.</p> </div> <div class="schema-faq-section" id="faq-question-1763007979641"><strong class="schema-faq-question">Can I add compound butter to the turkey cavity?</strong> <p class="schema-faq-answer">I&rsquo;ve found it&rsquo;s best to spread the butter under and over the skin rather than inside the cavity. That way, it melts evenly and keeps the skin golden instead of soggy.</p> </div> </div>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner has-background has-senary-background-color">

<h2 class="wp-block-heading block-tip__title" id="h-expert-tips-for-the-best-compound-butter-for-turkey">Expert Tips for the Best Compound Butter for Turkey</h2>



<p>If this is your first time making compound butter, these notes from several rounds of testing will help you get it just right every time:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Use softened butter:</strong> Let the butter sit at room temperature for about an hour, until it&rsquo;s soft and spreadable. If it&rsquo;s too firm, it won&rsquo;t blend smoothly with the herbs and seasoning.</li>



<li><strong>Rinse and dry your herbs:</strong> If you&rsquo;re using fresh herbs, wash and dry them thoroughly before mincing, as they can hold onto moisture, which makes the butter watery. I like to place the washed herbs between two pieces of paper towels to absorb excess moisture; alternatively, put them in a salad spinner to remove as much liquid as possible.</li>



<li><strong>Mince herbs finely:</strong> Chop your herbs as finely as possible to ensure they distribute evenly throughout the butter.&nbsp;</li>



<li><strong>A word on using fresh garlic and botulism: </strong>When using fresh garlic to make compound butter, keep in mind that a foodborne illness called botulism can occur if garlic is stored at temperatures between 50 &deg;F (10 &deg;C) and 113 &deg;F (45 &deg;C).&nbsp; Therefore, be sure to keep this seasoned butter in the refrigerator unless you plan to use it within the next hour or so.</li>
</ul>

</div>

